#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173164 :tutorial/content "A region $D$ is **connected** if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path that lies entirely in $D.$\n\nThe region below is connected because a smooth path can join any two points.\n\n![](images/90/90fea89e0dabec960edf7eef3552e10cdbccaee80f9ef6ca3d5b2a5b457e6b89.png)\n\nThe region below is not connected. If we take two points $P$ and $Q,$ one in each subregion, we cannot find a path joining these points that lies entirely in the region.\n\n![](images/08/08cd555c38106ec22f2ffe4808a6b62e8f72ffda7baf953836beeca5ee6a1faf.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "cc905518-dd4e-5791-9cf9-5655233a30a1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5105 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173189 :tutorial/content "A connected region $D$ is **simply connected** if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nWhen we have a connected *plane* region (e.g. a region in the $xy$-plane), determining whether it's simply connected is a little simpler: we just need to check whether the region contains holes. \n\nPlane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nSo, if a connected plane region does not contain any holes, then it is simply connected.\n\nMore precisely, a connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a2c2be37-32a5-5b2c-8386-ef9e32684998" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5574 :tutorial/title "Simply-Connected Regions"}]}
